# PR workflow (implementation tasks)

When the user asks to **implement**, **fix** (code change), **execute the plan**, or similar **explicit build intent** in this repo:

1. **Start from main**: `git fetch origin`, `git checkout main`, `git pull origin main`.
2. **Branch**: create `feature/<short-kebab-topic>` or `fix/<short-kebab-topic>` from `main`. Do not implement on `main`.
3. **Work**: make the agreed changes; run project-appropriate checks (e.g. sqlfluff, dbt compile) when edits warrant it.
4. **Ship**: commit; push with `git push -u origin HEAD`; open a PR with `gh pr create` targeting `main` (use `--body-file` on Windows PowerShell instead of bash heredocs).
5. **Reply** with the **PR link** and a short summary of what changed.

When the user says the PR is **merged** and asks for **housekeeping** (or equivalent):

1. `git fetch origin && git checkout main && git pull origin main`
2. Delete the merged local topic branch: `git branch -d <branch>` when safe
3. `git remote prune origin`
4. Optionally note any stale local branches (do not delete worktrees the user did not ask to remove).

This supplements [agent-behavior.mdc](agent-behavior.mdc): exploration and Q&A stay read-only until the user clearly asks for implementation.

## Fewer "Run" prompts in Cursor

Terminal approvals are controlled in **Cursor Settings** (e.g. **Agent** / **Chat** → auto-run or command allowlist). Enable auto-run only to the level you accept: the agent will still need network/sandbox approval for some environments. See Cursor’s Agent security docs for risks of `git push`, package installs, and destructive commands.
